# 7. Is it actually irrelevant whether a human likes the domain?

**Sometimes, almost yes. Universally, no.**

That's one of the more interesting conclusions.

### Case A — pure agent-facing API/MCP

Suppose:

```text
curlcheck.dev
factprobe.dev
velora.com
```

exists primarily so autonomous agents can discover and call an API.

The relevant funnel is:

```text
EXPOSED
   ↓
UNDERSTOOD
   ↓
SELECTED
   ↓
INVOKED
   ↓
VALID PARAMETERS
   ↓
TASK SUCCEEDED
   ↓
REUSED
```

If `factprobe.dev` wins that funnel but humans think `Velora` sounds cooler, the human preference may be practically irrelevant.

### Case B — B2B SaaS

Agents discover it.

Humans approve/pay/trust it.

Now both matter.

### Case C — consumer brand

Humans remember it, say it aloud, search it, share it.

Human preference/memory becomes dominant.

This is exactly why one universal score is wrong. Human and LLM preferences measurably decompose differently. ([arXiv][28])

DomainArena should therefore ask:

```text
WHO NEEDS TO CHOOSE THIS?

○ Consumers
○ Businesses
○ Developers
○ AI Agents
○ Mixed
```

and change **the experiment suite**, not merely change arbitrary numeric weights.

That is a much stronger product.

